        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ludwig van Beethoven</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1770 CE – 1827 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">Baptised on 17 December 1770 at the Catholic parish of St. Remigius in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>; the birth date is not documented, though there is a consensus, with which Beethoven himself agreed, that it was 16 December; son of Johann van Beethoven, a court tenor, and Maria Magdalena Keverich</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">26 March 1827 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, aged 56, after several months of illness; buried in the Währing cemetery after a funeral procession on 29 March attended by an estimated 10,000 people, and reinterred in 1888 at Vienna's Zentralfriedhof</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Court organist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> under Elector Maximilian Franz; from 1792 a composer and piano virtuoso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, supported by noble patrons including Prince Lichnowsky, Prince Lobkowitz and Archduke Rudolf; composer in residence at the Theater an der Wien (1803–1804)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">nine symphonies, including the Eroica, the Fifth and the Ninth; piano sonatas, among them the Pathétique, the Moonlight and the Hammerklavier; the string quartets, including the late quartets and the Große Fuge; the opera Fidelio; the Missa solemnis; the Diabelli Variations; composing through progressive deafness</span></div>
