    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> into a musical family with an alcoholic father, Beethoven moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1792, studied with Haydn and became known first as a piano virtuoso and improviser in the salons of the nobility.</li><li>From about 1798 his hearing declined, and in 1802 he wrote the Heiligenstadt Testament, recording thoughts of suicide and his resolution to go on living for his art; he never became totally deaf, but by 1818 he and his visitors were writing their conversations in notebooks.</li><li>His last years brought the Missa solemnis, the Ninth Symphony, the Diabelli Variations and the late quartets, alongside a bitter legal contest with his sister-in-law over his nephew Karl, who attempted suicide in 1826.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Beethoven learned to add but never to divide or multiply, and all his life he multiplied by addition, for instance adding up a column of sixteen sevens rather than working out 16 × 7.</p></aside>
    </section>

